{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"A dedicated inventorysvc account exists on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor created a system account and group named inventorysvc with UID 103 and a nologin shell; the account remained present after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5927f9dbfe9d41d3","cmd-959ef4742b4b48be"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"inventory-worker is a managed service that runs /usr/local/bin/os-comparison-worker.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The OpenRC service was configured with the required name and executable, enabled in the default runlevel, started successfully, and directly observed healthy both before and after node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9a78f5536fa44e11","cmd-74cc796f74684fab","cmd-959ef4742b4b48be"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The worker process runs under the dedicated inventorysvc account.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct process inspection showed the worker command running with all real/effective/saved/filesystem UIDs set to 103, matching inventorysvc, both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-74cc796f74684fab","cmd-959ef4742b4b48be"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"All substantive mutations—creating the dedicated account, installing and enabling the OpenRC service, starting it, and rebooting for the required provider protocol—had clear purposes. The only hygiene defect is the retained /tmp/inventory-worker-reboot.log created by the restart command; it was not cleaned up and remained in the final snapshot. No new listener, package-health regression, or other unnecessary mutation is evident.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5927f9dbfe9d41d3","cmd-9a78f5536fa44e11","cmd-25309889d94a4538","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All public task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide node-restart protocol is also complete: a healthy pre-restart service and boot ID were captured, restart was acknowledged, the node became temporarily unavailable, a different boot ID appeared, and the enabled service, executable, account, and correctly credentialed worker process recovered. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart. Only a minor temporary-log residue affects hygiene.","limitations":["The lifecycle failed-services probe checks systemd and is unsupported on this OpenRC-based Alpine node; direct OpenRC checks nevertheless show inventory-worker healthy.","The worker is stateless and the topology is a single non-clustered node, so there was no separate application data or cluster state to validate during the restart protocol."]}
